From: claude <claude@p7x.dev>
Subject: [PATCH v2 0/2] lab-process-mail: Add lab-process-maild
To: Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com>
Cc: lab-devel@p7x.dev
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 03:01:00 +0000

lab-process-mail answers the inbox only when someone runs it. This
series adds lab-process-maild, which keeps it running in the
background under daemon(8):

    lab-process-maild start
    lab-process-maild status
    lab-process-maild stop

It ships in the lab-process-mail package and finds its files under
the root the package is installed in, the parent of its bin/. After
"pkg -r ~/local" that is ~/local, and after a system install it is /,
so neither case needs a setting:

    etc/lab-process-maild.conf - settings, if present
    var/log/lab-process-maild.log - output of every pass
    var/run/lab-process-maild.pid - pid of daemon(8)

start clears the environment, keeping only HOME, USER, LOGNAME, and a
PATH that begins with the root's bin/. The conf is sh, and every
variable it sets is exported to lab-process-mail, for example:

    LAB_BASE_BRANCH='b/dev@upstream'
    NOTIFY_URL='https://ntfy.sh/my-topic'
    LAB_PROCESS_MAILD_INTERVAL=60

stop lets the pass in progress finish before the daemon exits. A
failed pass still stops the daemon; that behavior and how to fix it
are described in "[BUG] lab-process-mail gets stuck" [1].

Patch 1 adds the daemon. Patch 2 makes lab-process-mail answer one
message and exit; the daemon then runs it again at once while the
inbox has mail, and sleeps only once it is empty.

The tests run the real daemon(8) for start, status, and stop, and
stubs for the rest. kyua test passes, 26/26.

Changes in v2:
- Rename lab-process-mail-loop to lab-process-maild, as daemons are
  named.
- Drop the src/ prefix from the subjects.
- Default to a 60 second interval instead of 300.
- Add start, stop, and status, so the daemon(8) invocation is no
  longer the operator's to type.
- Start from a clean environment, set up by etc/lab-process-maild.conf.
- Log to var/log/ and write the pidfile to var/run/ under the install
  root.
- New patch 2: lab-process-mail no longer loops over the inbox.
